  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 255 PM MDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nThe long term period is shaping up to be fairly consistent with\nunseasonably hot temperatures and evening storm chances due to \nour region being in a persistent southwest upper-level flow. \nMonday and Tuesday, high temperatures are forecast in the 80s to\nupper 90s. Wednesday through Friday, highs are currently \nforecast in the high 90s with LREF ensemble members showing at \nleast a 20% chance of high exceeding 100 degrees F for our \nKansas and Nebraska counties. \n\nOur persistent southwest upper-level flow will send several \nembedded shortwaves, bringing precipitation chances each evening\nMonday through Friday. Monday is looking to be out best chance \nfor precipitation with Probability of Precipitation (PoPs) from \n30-60%, increasing from east to west. Models are showing a \nmarginally unstable environment with less than 1000 J/Kg of \nCAPE, around 30 knots of shear and mid-low level lapse rates \nless than 7 C/km. Model soundings are showing precipitable water\n(PWAT) values greater than 1 inch and overall skinny CAPE \nprofiles, so heavy rainfall and localized flooding are possible \nwith severe or sub- severe storms. Small hail and strong winds \ncould be a concern with stronger storms if they are able to \nform. Tuesday onward, evening PoPs are around 20% for the area \nwith low confidence in severe weather occurring. \n\nDespite unseasonably hot temperatures, fire weather concerns \nare minimal through the long term period. Relative humidity (RH)\nvalues are forecast above 20% for the CWA. Winds will remain \noverall light with gusts maxing out around 20 mph.",
